load @term/seed/code/structure/set
  find set

# The universe hierarchy, the spine of the type theory. A single self-typed universe (a type of all types including
# itself) is inconsistent by Girard's paradox, so types are stratified into a tower. The full set of rules below makes
# it predicative, cumulative, and universe-polymorphic, with an impredicative bottom.

# A LEVEL: the address of a universe, a constant floor joined with the maximum over level variables each carried with a
# non-negative offset. This algebra (the same as Agda's and Lean's) makes level comparison decidable, which universe
# polymorphism needs. A level may be a literal (Type 0, Type 1) or polymorphic (Type i for a variable i).
form level
  link floor, like set
  link variables, like set

# FORMATION and TYPING: Type at a level is a type, and Type i has type Type (i + 1). No universe is its own type. This
# is the rule that replaces Type-in-Type and restores consistency.
form universe
  link level, like level

# CUMULATIVITY: a type in a lower universe is also a type in every higher universe, so a small type is usable wherever a
# larger one is expected, without explicit lifting. Type i is contained in Type j whenever i is at most j.
form cumulativity
  link level, like level

# UNIVERSE POLYMORPHISM: a definition may be written once over a level variable and instantiated at every level, so a
# notion like "the identity function" or "lists" exists uniformly at all sizes instead of being copied per level.
form universe-polymorphism
  link level, like level

# IMPREDICATIVE BOTTOM: a function type (a pi) whose codomain lands in Type 0 stays in Type 0 whatever the domain's
# level. Only the literal bottom is impredicative; every universe above stays predicative, and Type 0 has type Type 1
# unchanged. This impredicative-Set discipline is what lets a self-encoded inductive live in the bottom universe rather
# than floating above its own induction motive.
form impredicative-bottom
  link level, like level
